\section{Method}\label{sec:method}

\subsection{Problem Formulation}\label{sec:formulation}

Given model $\mathcal{M}$ and input $x$, we sample $N$ outputs $\{y_1, \ldots, y_N\}$ at temperature $T > 0$ via constrained decoding \cite{willard2023guided}, where each $y_i$ is an entity set.
A \textbf{per-sample signal} $c_{\text{ps}}: (x, y_i) \to \mathbb{R}$ scores each output independently; an \textbf{instance-level signal} $c_{\text{il}}: (x, \{y_i\}_{i=1}^N) \to \mathbb{R}$ produces a single score per input (notation in Appendix~\ref{app:notation}).
This distinction is consequential for selection: in our study, 4 of 5 signals are instance-level aggregates that assign identical QE scores to all $N$ candidates. Consensus-proximity (MBR) converts these to per-sample rankings (\S\ref{sec:selection_gap}), but these rankings do not improve selection; only natively per-sample signals can, in principle, exploit oracle headroom.

\subsection{Confidence Signals}\label{sec:signals}

Four are \textit{consistency signals}: \textit{structural} (Soft Jaccard [SJ], entity voting confidence [VC]) and \textit{surface} (exact match [EM], Fleiss' $\kappa$ [FK]); log-probability [LP] is the sole \textit{model-internal}, per-sample signal.

\paragraph{Log-probability (model-internal, per-sample).}
For sample $y_i$ with tokens $t_1, \ldots, t_{|y_i|}$:
\begin{equation}\label{eq:logprob}
c_{\text{lp}}(y_i) = \frac{1}{|y_i|} \sum_{j=1}^{|y_i|} \log p_{\mathcal{M}}(t_j \mid t_{<j}, x)
\end{equation}

\paragraph{Exact match voting (surface, instance-level).}
The proportion of samples producing the most common output:
\begin{equation}\label{eq:em}
c_{\text{em}}(x) = \frac{1}{N} \max_{y} \big|\{i : y_i = y\}\big|
\end{equation}

\paragraph{Fleiss' $\kappa$ (surface, instance-level).}
We adapt \citet{fleiss1971agreement} to entity sets: items are unique (text, type) tuples across all $N$ samples; each sample assigns binary present/absent labels, yielding a $|E_\cup| \times 2$ rating matrix:
\begin{equation}\label{eq:fk}
\kappa = \frac{\bar{P} - \bar{P}_e}{1 - \bar{P}_e}
\end{equation}
where $\bar{P}$ is mean observed and $\bar{P}_e$ expected agreement.
Temperature-based sampling introduces mild negative dependence ($-$0.4\% to $-$4.0\%; Appendix~\ref{app:fk_independence}), which does not inflate FK.
Unlike structural signals, FK relies on exact string matching.

\paragraph{Soft Jaccard (structural, instance-level).}
For samples $y_i, y_j$, we group extractions by type, compute max-weight bipartite matching (Hungarian algorithm, token-level IoU), and define:
\begin{equation}\label{eq:sj}
\text{SJ}(y_i, y_j) = \frac{\sum_{m \in M} \text{sim}(a_m, b_m)}{\sum_{g} \max(|g_i|, |g_j|)}
\end{equation}
where $M$ is the matched set, $\text{sim}(a_m, b_m)$ is the token-level IoU, and $\max(|g_i|, |g_j|)$ penalizes length asymmetry.\footnote{The two normalizer variants produce nearly identical rankings ($|\Delta\rho|{\leq}0.009$; Table~\ref{tab:sj_normalizer}).}
The instance-level QE signal averages over all pairs: $c_{\text{sj}}(x) = \binom{N}{2}^{-1} \sum_{i<j} \text{SJ}(y_i, y_j)$.
For selection, consensus-proximity converts this to per-sample utility $u_{\text{sj}}(y_i) = (N{-}1)^{-1} \sum_{j \neq i} \text{SJ}(y_i, y_j)$, equivalent to MBR decoding \cite{eikema2022sampling}. The same construction applies to FK, EM, and VC.

\paragraph{Entity voting confidence (structural, instance-level).}
For each entity $e$ in the union $E_\cup$ of all samples, we compute frequency $\text{freq}(e) = |\{i : e \in y_i\}| / N$.
The instance-level confidence is the mean frequency:
\begin{equation}\label{eq:voting}
c_{\text{vote}}(x) = \frac{1}{|E_\cup|} \sum_{e \in E_\cup} \text{freq}(e)
\end{equation}
When $E_\cup = \emptyset$, $c_{\mathrm{vc}}(x) = 0$.\footnote{For voted-output \textit{selection}, we include only entities with frequency ${>}N/2$ (standard majority voting).}

\subsection{Evaluation Framework}\label{sec:evaluation}

\paragraph{Quality estimation.}
Spearman $\rho$ between the signal and instance-level F1, and AUROC classifying instances as above/below median F1.
Both quality estimation and selection exclude instances with no gold annotations: $n{=}529$ on SciERC (from 551), $n{=}2{,}756$ on CoNLL (from 3{,}453).\footnote{On CoNLL, full-set ($n{=}3{,}453$) Spearman $\rho$ values are higher than gold-filtered ($n{=}2{,}756$) by $+0.03$--$0.05$ due to the zero/non-zero boundary inflating correlations, paralleling the effect documented for WNUT-17 in Appendix~\ref{app:additional}.}

\paragraph{Sample selection.}
Best-of-$N$ F1: per-sample signals (LP) directly rank candidates; instance-level signals rank via consensus-proximity (MBR utility; see SJ definition above).
Baselines: \textit{oracle} (highest-F1 sample) and \textit{greedy} ($T{=}0$ argmax decoding).
We define \textbf{instance degeneracy} as the fraction of instances where all $N$ samples yield identical F1; for these instances, no signal can improve selection.
We report F1 under the protocol matching each experiment's configuration (seed, epoch, $N$). SciERC greedy F1 appears as four values across tables (0.640, 0.644, 0.645, 0.650) due to protocol variants: primary seed vs.\ multi-seed mean, 5-epoch vs.\ 3-epoch checkpoint, and gold-filtered vs.\ full test set. Each table specifies its protocol.

%% Prescriptive framework content is in Discussion §5 (sec:selection_method label there)
%% to stay within 8-page main body limit

\section{Experiments}
\label{sec:experiments}

\subsection{Experimental Setup}
\label{sec:setup}

\paragraph{Datasets.}
We evaluate on four English NER benchmarks spanning a difficulty gradient, each serving a distinct role.
\textbf{SciERC} \cite{luan2018scierc} (551 test instances, 6 entity types) is the primary medium-difficulty test bed where models achieve ${\approx}$65\% F1.
\textbf{Few-NERD} \cite{ding2021fewnerd} (INTRA coarse; 37{,}648 instances, 8 entity types, ${\approx}$75\% F1) provides a low-degeneracy contrast where LP selection succeeds (\S\ref{sec:fewnerd}).
\textbf{CoNLL-2003} \cite{tjongkimsang2003conll} (3{,}453 test sentences, 4 entity types, ${\approx}$91\% F1) serves as a ceiling-effect control where limited oracle headroom constrains selection gains.
\textbf{WNUT-17} \cite{derczynski2017wnut} (1{,}287 social media instances, 52\% zero F1) probes the signal-collapse boundary where model capability is insufficient.

\paragraph{Models and sampling.}
Both \textbf{Qwen3-8B} and \textbf{LLaMA-3.1-8B-Instruct} are fine-tuned with LoRA (rank$=$32, $\alpha{=}64$) for 5 epochs using LLaMA Factory \cite{zheng2024llamafactory} under identical training procedures, except Few-NERD models which use 3 epochs (early stopping at the best validation checkpoint; a 5-epoch control confirms LP selection remains positive though gains are halved; Appendix~\ref{app:epoch_confound}).
Qwen3-8B's SciERC checkpoint has a minor train--test mismatch (empty thinking tokens); a quantitative analysis rules out this confound (Appendix~\ref{app:thinking_token}).
We draw $N{=}8$ samples at $T{=}1.0$ (primary), with $N{=}16$ for scaling experiments (ranking stability $\sigma{\leq}0.026$, Table~\ref{tab:multiseed}; sublinear gains beyond $N{=}8$, Appendix~\ref{app:n_scaling}).
Inference uses vLLM with constrained decoding \cite{willard2023guided} (seed protocol: Appendix~\ref{app:multiseed}).

%% ============================================================
\subsection{Quality Estimation Results}
\label{sec:qe_results}

\begin{table*}[t]
\centering
\small
\caption{NER quality estimation ($\rho$ and AUROC; $N{=}8$, primary seed${=}42$; 3-seed stability $\sigma{\leq}0.026$ in Table~\ref{tab:multiseed}). Best in \textbf{bold}. RE results in Appendix~\ref{app:re_extension}. $N{=}16$ in Table~\ref{tab:robustness}.}
\label{tab:quality_estimation}
\begin{tabular}{ll cc cc cc}
\toprule
& & \multicolumn{2}{c}{\textbf{SciERC}} & \multicolumn{2}{c}{\textbf{CoNLL (Qwen)}} & \multicolumn{2}{c}{\textbf{CoNLL (LLaMA)}} \\
\cmidrule(lr){3-4} \cmidrule(lr){5-6} \cmidrule(lr){7-8}
Signal & Family & $\rho$ & AUC & $\rho$ & AUC & $\rho$ & AUC \\
\midrule
VC & Structural & \textbf{.379} & \textbf{.663} & .424 & .789 & .426 & .709 \\
SJ  & Structural & .360 & .650 & .436 & .801 & \textbf{.431} & .704 \\
FK & Surface & .267 & .602 & .400 & .774 & .428 & .703 \\
EM  & Surface    & .295 & .614 & \textbf{.451} & \textbf{.816} & \textbf{.431} & .705 \\
\midrule
LP     & Model      & .205 & .578 & .316 & .753 & .307 & \textbf{.761} \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table*}

Table~\ref{tab:quality_estimation} presents $\rho$ and AUROC for five signals across tasks and models ($\varepsilon$-free; $\varepsilon{=}0.05$ applies to selection only, stable across $[0, 0.2]$, Appendix~\ref{app:epsilon_robustness}).

\paragraph{Signal rankings are difficulty-dependent.}
On SciERC (medium difficulty), structural signals lead (VC $\rho{=}0.379$, SJ $0.360$) over LP ($0.205$); bootstrap CIs confirm the gap ($\Delta\rho{=}{+}0.155$; Appendix~\ref{app:bootstrap_signal}).
On CoNLL (single-sample F1${\approx}$0.91), EM leads both $\rho$ and AUROC ($0.816$).
At the signal-collapse boundary (WNUT-17, macro F1${\approx}$42\%), full-set correlations are dominated by the zero/non-zero boundary (VC $\rho{=}0.724$ but conditional $\rho{=}0.038$; Table~\ref{tab:wnut}); LP shows the reverse (conditional $\rho{=}0.350$; Figure~\ref{fig:signal_heatmap}).

%% ============================================================
\subsection{The Correlation-Selection Gap}
\label{sec:selection_gap}

Two factors constrain selection: instance degeneracy collapses the candidate pool for consensus-based approaches, and weak LP-quality alignment prevents exploiting the remaining headroom.
Four instance-level signals cannot rank candidates by construction; LP selection effectiveness is primarily determined by LP-quality alignment rather than degeneracy level (\S\ref{sec:discussion}).

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\small
\caption{Best-of-$N$ NER selection (macro F1) on SciERC ($N{=}8$, $T{=}1.0$, 2-seed mean${\pm}\sigma$). LP is per-sample; others use consensus-proximity. Best non-oracle in \textbf{bold}. Non-tied subset in \S\ref{sec:discussion}; RE in Appendix~\ref{app:re_extension}.}
\label{tab:selection}
\begin{tabular}{lcc}
\toprule
Method & NER F1 & $p$ \\
\midrule
Oracle & $0.775 \pm 0.001$ & --- \\
Greedy ($T{=}0$) & $0.645 \pm 0.001$ & --- \\
\midrule
\multicolumn{3}{l}{\textit{Per-sample signal}} \\
LP           & $\mathbf{0.647} \pm 0.005$ & .29 \\
\midrule
\multicolumn{3}{l}{\textit{Instance-level signals (consensus-proximity selection)}} \\
SJ           & $0.646 \pm 0.002$ & .67 \\
FK           & $0.646 \pm 0.001$ & .79 \\
EM           & $0.637 \pm 0.001$ & --- \\
VC           & $0.623 \pm 0.004$ & --- \\
\midrule
Random           & $0.610 \pm 0.003$ & --- \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\columnwidth]{fig_selection_gap}
\caption{The correlation-selection gap on SciERC NER ($N{=}8$, $T{=}1.0$, 2-seed mean). Bars show best-of-$N$ selection F1; the line marks quality estimation $\rho$. Despite $\rho$ up to 0.40, no signal significantly outperforms greedy (all $p{>}0.05$; Table~\ref{tab:selection}). Dashed line: oracle upper bound.}
\label{fig:selection_gap}
\end{figure}

Despite moderate quality estimation, no signal consistently improves selection over greedy (Table~\ref{tab:selection}; Figure~\ref{fig:selection_gap}).
Instance-level signals assign identical QE scores to all candidates; MBR conversion does not improve over greedy on the primary test bed.

\paragraph{No significant improvement over greedy.}
On SciERC ($n{=}529$, 2-seed sign tests\footnote{MDE${\approx}$1.5~pp at 80\% power ($n{=}529$); Few-NERD ($n{=}32{,}565$; MDE${\approx}$0.22~pp) provides the definitive test.}), the oracle (0.775) reveals 13.0~pp of headroom, but LP achieves $+0.2$~pp ($p{=}0.29$), below the MDE (${\approx}$1.5~pp).
The pattern holds across CoNLL and WNUT-17 (Table~\ref{tab:cross_selection}); under stringent TOST anchors, some configurations show significant \textit{degradation} (Appendix~\ref{app:tost}).
Few-NERD provides the definitive contrast: LP achieves $+1.39$~pp ($p{<}0.001$) at low degeneracy (\S\ref{sec:fewnerd}), while structural signals still degrade selection.
Instance-level signals predict \textit{which inputs} the model handles reliably, not \textit{which output} is best; closing this gap likely requires trained verifiers \cite{cobbe2021verifiers} (the sole positive finding, non-tied LP subset $+2.9$~pp $p{=}0.004$, survives Bonferroni correction; \S\ref{sec:discussion}).

%% ============================================================
\subsection{Cross-Dataset Contrast: Few-NERD}
\label{sec:fewnerd}

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\small
\caption{LP selection across datasets (Qwen3-8B, $N{=}8$, $T{=}1.0$, gold-filtered). Instance degeneracy (Degen.\%): fraction where all $N$ samples yield identical F1. $p$: paired bootstrap ($n{=}10{,}000$) vs.\ greedy. Few-NERD and CoNLL $\Delta$ values are 3-seed means; SciERC is single-seed (3-seed mean: $+0.04{\pm}0.48$~pp). Oracle headroom: 5.0~pp (CoNLL), 13.0~pp (SciERC), 12.4~pp (Few-NERD). Minor greedy F1 variations across tables reflect protocol differences (\S\ref{sec:evaluation}). Few-NERD uses 3-epoch checkpoints; SciERC and CoNLL use 5 epochs (Appendix~\ref{app:epoch_confound}).}
\label{tab:fewnerd}
\resizebox{\columnwidth}{!}{%
\begin{tabular}{@{}lrccccr@{}}
\toprule
Dataset & $n$ & Greedy & Degen.\% & LP $\rho$ & LP $\Delta$ & $p$ \\
\midrule
CoNLL & 2{,}756 & .908 & 48.4\% & .302 & $-$0.85\,pp & 1.0 \\
SciERC & 529 & .644 & 17.8\% & .205 & $+$0.61\,pp & .193 \\
Few-NERD & 32{,}565 & .748 & 13.8\% & .220 & $\mathbf{+1.39}$\,pp & ${<}.001$ \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}}
\end{table}

Few-NERD (Table~\ref{tab:fewnerd}) confirms that LP selection effectiveness is inversely associated with degeneracy.
Under standard early stopping (3 epochs for Few-NERD vs.\ 5 for SciERC/CoNLL), Few-NERD degeneracy is 13.8\%, and LP achieves $+1.39$~pp ($p{<}0.001$, seed $\sigma{=}0.04$~pp vs.\ SJ $\sigma{=}2.02$~pp), while all structural signals degrade selection (VC $-1.79$, SJ $-2.14$~pp).
LP selection is 50$\times$ more stable across random seeds than structural selection, making it the only practically reliable signal for deployment scenarios requiring reproducibility.
LLaMA-3.1-8B confirms the pattern: LP is the only positive signal ($+0.36$~pp) despite higher degeneracy ($23.4$\% vs.\ Qwen $12.7$\%), while all structural signals degrade selection (VC $-2.39$~pp; Table~\ref{tab:cross_selection}).
Training convergence modulates degeneracy: a 5-epoch Few-NERD control raises degeneracy to 31\% and halves LP gains, though selection remains positive (Appendix~\ref{app:epoch_confound}).

\paragraph{Degeneracy alone does not predict LP selection direction.}
Across 23 dataset-model-temperature configurations at $N{=}8$, degeneracy does not predict LP gain ($\rho{=}0.052$, $p{=}0.81$; Figure~\ref{fig:degeneracy_scatter}).
Counter-examples illustrate: LLaMA CoNLL exhibits 86.5\% degeneracy yet LP is non-negative ($+0.14$~pp), while a 3-epoch SciERC model at 6.4\% degeneracy yields LP $-1.18$~pp.
Within fixed settings, however, degeneracy causally modulates LP effectiveness: the SciERC epoch ablation ($-1.18$, $+0.74$, $+0.99$~pp at 3, 5, 10 epochs; Appendix~\ref{app:epoch_confound}) and per-entity-type analysis within Few-NERD ($n{=}8$, $\rho{=}{-}0.881$, $p{=}0.004$; Appendix~\ref{app:per_type_lp}) both show degeneracy effects when dataset and model are held constant.
Cross-dataset prediction requires LP signal quality: Few-NERD (within-instance $\rho{\approx}0.5$--$0.7$) shows consistent gains, while SciERC ($\rho{\approx}0.26$) and CoNLL ($\rho{\approx}0.51$, but only 41\% non-degenerate) show mixed results.
Even at matched training duration (3 epochs on both datasets), SciERC within-instance $\rho{=}0.319$ remains below Few-NERD's ${\sim}0.5$ (Table~\ref{tab:epoch_scierc}), confirming that LP-quality alignment, not training duration, drives the cross-dataset difference.
All five signals achieve positive QE on Few-NERD (VC $\rho{=}0.39$, LP $0.22$), yet only LP translates QE into selection; the gap confirms that strong instance-level QE cannot rank candidates within an instance.
Conditional analysis on the favorable subset (non-degenerate instances whose within-instance LP range exceeds the dataset-specific median) yields $+2.47$~pp on SciERC (CI $[+0.30, +4.70]$) and a marginal positive trend on Few-NERD ($+0.63$~pp, CI $[-0.02, +1.30]$); CoNLL remains negative ($-1.78$~pp), consistent with ceiling-regime degeneracy even within the favorable subset (Appendix~\ref{app:conditional_decomp}).

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\columnwidth]{figures/fig_degeneracy_lp_scatter.pdf}
\caption{Instance degeneracy vs.\ LP selection gain across 23 dataset-model-temperature configurations ($N{=}8$; $\rho{=}0.052$, $p{=}0.81$): degeneracy alone does not predict LP direction across datasets. Within Few-NERD per-entity-type analysis ($n{=}8$; $\rho{=}{-}0.881$, $p{=}0.004$; Appendix~\ref{app:per_type_lp}), degeneracy predicts LP gain when LP alignment is held constant. Selection viability depends on LP signal quality and dataset characteristics, not degeneracy alone.}
\label{fig:degeneracy_scatter}
\end{figure}

\paragraph{LP selection scales with sample budget.}
Doubling $N$ from 8 to 16 on Few-NERD yields $+0.52$~pp (paired $p{=}0.003$, CI $[+0.17, +0.87]$), driven by reduced degeneracy (14.6\% $\to$ 7.0\%) expanding headroom (12.1 $\to$ 15.4~pp), while LP ranking quality remains stable ($\rho{\approx}0.62$).
Structural signals remain uniformly negative at $N{=}16$; the gap persists regardless of sample budget.

%% ============================================================
\subsection{Robustness}
\label{sec:robustness_main}

Consistency signals are robust across $T{\in}\{0.7, 1.0\}$ with sublinear $N$-scaling gains (Table~\ref{tab:robustness}); LP shows the largest temperature sensitivity ($\Delta\rho{=}{+}0.044$).
Signal rankings are stable across seeds (Kendall's $W{=}1.0$ at $N{=}8$; $m{=}3$ raters, interpret with caution given low $m$; exact $p{\approx}0.028$; Table~\ref{tab:ranking-stability}).
NER calibration is reasonable (ECE 0.069--0.096; Appendix~\ref{app:calibration}).

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\small
\caption{Scale ablation: Qwen3-8B vs.\ 4B NER quality estimation ($\rho$; $N{=}8$, $T{=}1.0$, seed${=}42$). 8B values identical to Table~\ref{tab:quality_estimation}; 4B uses matched evaluation protocol. RE scale ablation in Appendix~\ref{app:re_extension}.}
\label{tab:scale_ablation}
\begin{tabular}{@{}ll cc r@{}}
\toprule
& & \textbf{8B} & \textbf{4B} & $\Delta$ \\
\midrule
CoNLL & SJ & .436 & .422 & $-$.014 \\
      & FK & .400 & .418 & $+$.018 \\
SciERC & SJ & .360 & .422 & $+$.062 \\
       & FK & .267 & .326 & $+$.059 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

\paragraph{Scale robustness.}
With Qwen3-4B (Table~\ref{tab:scale_ablation}), QE is comparable (mean $|\Delta\rho|{=}0.042$), yet selection remains below greedy despite 13.0~pp headroom. Task difficulty is a stronger predictor than model scale within the 3B--8B range (Appendix~\ref{app:llama3b}).
Degeneracy scales with capability: 8B shows 17.8\% vs.\ 4B's 10.4\% on SciERC (Table~\ref{tab:degeneracy}).

\paragraph{LoRA-rank ablation.}
Training with LoRA rank${=}8$ (vs.\ 32) lowers degeneracy from 17.8\% to 7.2\% and increases headroom to 16.0~pp, but LP does not improve selection (0.635 vs.\ greedy 0.640).
This ablation conflates diversity with model capacity (rank 8 has fewer trainable parameters); within-instance $\rho(\text{LP}, \text{F1}){\approx}0.24$ remains too weak despite wider LP spread (0.057 vs.\ 0.041 nats).
Temperature manipulation (\S\ref{sec:discussion}) provides a cleaner test by altering diversity without changing capacity, and yields the same conclusion: both low degeneracy and adequate alignment appear independently associated with selection success.

\paragraph{Supervised signal combination.}
Supervised classifiers (logistic regression, MLP, random forest) trained on all five signals (5-fold CV, 3-seed mean, SciERC, $N{=}16$, $+17.9$~pp headroom) all perform at or below greedy (F1${=}0.644$; LR: $-0.4$~pp; MLP: $-1.6$~pp; RF: $-3.1$~pp).
Adding pairwise interaction features does not help (AUROC 0.634 vs.\ 0.638).
To test whether richer representations could close the gap, we train a DeBERTa-v3-base verifier that encodes input-output pairs and predicts per-sample F1.
DeBERTa achieves $\rho{=}0.329{\pm}0.087$ (5-fold CV, 3 seeds). Despite moderate quality prediction, selection utility is poor: on SciERC, DeBERTa closes $-26.5{\pm}26.5$\% of the oracle gap (13 of 15 runs negative; vs.\ LP's $+4.2{\pm}10.7$\%); on Few-NERD, DeBERTa recovers only $+3.5$\% (single 80/20 split; vs.\ LP's $+16.3$\%).
Cross-dataset transfer fails entirely ($-27$\% to $-72$\% gap closure).
Neither handcrafted signal combinations (LR, MLP, RF on 5 scalar signals) nor learned text representations close the selection gap; only model-internal log-probability provides meaningful selection signal, consistent with an information asymmetry between the generating model and external observers.

